h="13.81"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.T2.pic8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1.5}{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn mathsize="0.500em">1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.500em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn mathsize="0.500em">5</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.500em">ϵ</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1.5}{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 146.62 23.52 L 151.57 23.52"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.32pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 146.62 23.52)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -1.66 2.21 C -1.52 1.38 0 0.14 0.42 0 C 0 -0.14 -1.52 -1.38 -1.66 -2.21"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.32pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 151.57 23.52)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -1.66 2.21 C -1.52 1.38 0 0.14 0.42 0 C 0 -0.14 -1.52 -1.38 -1.66 -2.21"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 145.93 28.41)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:7.36pt;" height="2.98" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 2.98)" width="4.24"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.T2.pic8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.500em">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 156.36 19.01 L 156.36 0.64"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.32pt" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 156.36 19.01)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -1.66 2.21 C -1.52 1.38 0 0.14 0.42 0 C 0 -0.14 -1.52 -1.38 -1.66 -2.21"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.32pt" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 156.36 0.64)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -1.66 2.21 C -1.52 1.38 0 0.14 0.42 0 C 0 -0.14 -1.52 -1.38 -1.66 -2.21"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 159.14 4.89)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.31em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:7.36pt;" height="3.12" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 3.12)" width="13.81"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.T2.pic8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{0.5}{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn mathsize="0.500em">0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.500em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn mathsize="0.500em">5</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.500em">ϵ</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{0.5}{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
691:<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An instance of <math id="S3.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gcircuit</span> with the corresponding construction for <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Chalving</span>.</figcaption>
696:<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the construction of a <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> instance above, for <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\min\{\epsilon/11,1/40\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">¡</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\min\{\epsilon/11,1/40\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a partition with <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cuts corresponds to a solution to <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gcircuit</span>.</span></p>
702:<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">First observe that since all of the agents <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="var_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">var_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="copy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">copy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are constructed to have at least <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3/4</annotation></semantics></math> of their valuation on <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i},d_{i}+3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i},d_{i}+3]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i}+3,d_{i}+6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i}+3,d_{i}+6]</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, there must be at least one cut in each one of those intervals in any <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-approximate solution to Consensus-halving (with <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/4</annotation></semantics></math>) and therefore any <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-approximate solution to Consensus-halving with <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> cuts must have exactly one cut in each interval. Furthermore, since the constructed instance consists of <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> agents, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, such a partition with <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> cuts is guaranteed to exist.</span></p>
705:<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider such a solution <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> with <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> cuts. For each agent <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="var_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">var_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (and associated gate <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, if any), since her valuation in <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least the same as her valuation outside the interval <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i},d_{i}+3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i},d_{i}+3]</annotation></semantics></math>, the cut from <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i},d_{i}+3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i},d_{i}+3]</annotation></semantics></math> must be in <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i}+1-\epsilon^{\prime},d_{i}+2+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i}+1-\epsilon^{\prime},d_{i}+2+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution to <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>. We will assume without loss of generality that the leftmost piece of the partition <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice then that for each node <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the piece on the left-hand side of the cut in <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is always in <math id="S3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and the piece on the left-hand side of the cut in <math id="S3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is always in <math id="S3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
706:Let the location of the cut be <math id="S3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},1+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},1+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Analogously, the same argument holds for agent <math id="S3.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="copy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">copy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the interval <math id="S3.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i}+3-\epsilon^{\prime},d_{i}+4+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i}+3-\epsilon^{\prime},d_{i}+4+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="S3.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}^{+}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},1+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}^{+}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},1+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> similarly.</span></p>
709:<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider the agent <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="copy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">copy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the cut at location <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}^{-}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}^{-}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, then since agent <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="copy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">copy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has valuation <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on interval <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> of her valuation will be on a piece in <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-t_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-t_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> of her valuation will be on a piece in <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, since <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution to <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>, the cut in <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}+3+t_{i}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}+3+t_{i}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> must be placed so that <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|t_{i}^{-}-t_{i}^{+}|\leq\epsilon^{\prime}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|t_{i}^{-}-t_{i}^{+}|\leq\epsilon^{\prime}/2</annotation></semantics></math>; similarly for the cases where <math id="S3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}^{-}\notin[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}^{-}\notin[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="copy_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">copy_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that the cut at <math id="S3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is “copied” <math id="S3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-approximately.</span></p>
712:<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will interpret the solution <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> as a solution to <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gcircuit</span> in the following way. For each node <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and each associated cut at <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}+1+t_{i}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> let</span></p>
730:<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|t_{i}^{+}-x_{i}|\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}\ \ \ ,\ \ \ |t_{i}^{-}-x_{i}|\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.5em;" width="1.5em"></mspace></mrow><mo rspace="1.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|t_{i}^{+}-x_{i}|\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}\ \ \ ,\ \ \ |t_{i}^{-}-x_{i}|\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
737:<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Constant gate</em> <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{\zeta},nil,nil,v_{out},\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>ζ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{\zeta},nil,nil,v_{out},\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>: The valuation of agent <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="var_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">var_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> for the intervals <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i},d_{i}+1+\alpha]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i},d_{i}+1+\alpha]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i}+1+\alpha,d_{i}+3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i}+1+\alpha,d_{i}+3]</annotation></semantics></math> is the same and since the height of the agent’s value density function is at least 1 in <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{i},d_{i}+3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{i},d_{i}+3]</annotation></semantics></math>,<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
741:            Notice that the constant gate is the only gate where <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="border_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">border_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> overlap.</span></span></span> it holds that <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by Equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it holds that <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[\alpha-3\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha+3\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ3</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[\alpha-3\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha+3\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, so for <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/3</annotation></semantics></math> the gate constraint is satisfied.</p>
744:<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Multiplication-by-scalar gate</em> <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{\times\zeta},v_{in},nil,v_{out},\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{\times\zeta},v_{in},nil,v_{out},\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that for any given cut <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> as the height of <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{out}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{out}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least 1. Similarly, for the case <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and any given cut <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+(1-\alpha)/2-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+(1-\alpha)/2+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+(1-\alpha)/2-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+(1-\alpha)/2+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> as the height of <math id="S3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{out}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{out}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least 1. In particular, since <math id="S3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\alpha&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\alpha&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, it also holds that <math id="S3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2-\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> for this case as well. Then, by Equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it holds that <math id="S3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2-3\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2+3\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2-3\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha t_{in}^{+}+\epsilon/2+3\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> and since <math id="S3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> it also holds that <math id="S3.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[\alpha x_{in}+\epsilon/2-5\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha x_{in}+\epsilon/2+5\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[\alpha x_{in}+\epsilon/2-5\epsilon^{\prime},\alpha x_{in}+\epsilon/2+5\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, again by Equation <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Then the gate constraint is satisfied whenever <math id="S3.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
747:<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Addition gate</em> <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{{+}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{{+}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)</annotation></semantics></math>. If for the cuts <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{2}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{2}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> it holds that <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&lt;1-\epsilon+4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&lt;1-\epsilon+4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}-5\epsilon^{\prime},t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}+5\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ5</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}-5\epsilon^{\prime},t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}+5\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> as the height of <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{out}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{out}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least 1. This then implies that <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[x_{in_{1}}^{+}+x_{in_{2}}^{+}-11\epsilon^{\prime},x_{in_{1}}^{+}+x_{in_{2}}^{+}+11\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ11</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[x_{in_{1}}^{+}+x_{in_{2}}^{+}-11\epsilon^{\prime},x_{in_{1}}^{+}+x_{in_{2}}^{+}+11\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. On the other hand, when <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}\geq 1-\epsilon+4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}\geq 1-\epsilon+4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it holds that <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[1-\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[1-\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and clearly <math id="S3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> which by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implies that <math id="S3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. The gate constraints are satisfied for <math id="S3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11</annotation></semantics></math> for each of the cases.</p>
750:<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Subtraction gate</em> <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{{-}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{{-}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)</annotation></semantics></math>. Analogously to the addition gate described above, when for the cuts <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> it holds that <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{+}&gt;\epsilon-4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ4</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{+}&gt;\epsilon-4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{+}-5\epsilon^{\prime},t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}+5\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ5</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{+}-5\epsilon^{\prime},t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}+5\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> as the height of <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{out}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{out}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least 1. This implies that <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[x_{in_{1}}^{+}-x_{in_{2}}^{-}-11\epsilon^{\prime},x_{in_{1}}^{+}-x_{in_{2}}^{-}+11\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ11</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[x_{in_{1}}^{+}-x_{in_{2}}^{-}-11\epsilon^{\prime},x_{in_{1}}^{+}-x_{in_{2}}^{-}+11\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. On the other hand when <math id="S3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}\leq\epsilon-\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}\leq\epsilon-\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[0,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[0,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math> by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it clearly holds that <math id="S3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have <math id="S3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>. The gate constraints are satisfied for <math id="S3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11</annotation></semantics></math> for each of the cases.</p>
753:<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Less-than-equal gate</em> <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{{&lt;}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>¡</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{{&lt;}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)</annotation></semantics></math>. We will consider three cases, depending on the positions of the cuts <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{2}}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{2}}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, when <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}|&lt;\epsilon-4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ4</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}|&lt;\epsilon-4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> it holds that <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x_{in_{1}}-x_{in_{2}}|&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x_{in_{1}}-x_{in_{2}}|&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and the output of the gate is unconstrained. When <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}\geq\epsilon-4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ4</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}-t_{in_{2}}^{-}\geq\epsilon-4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> then by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> it holds that <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}}\geq x_{in_{2}}+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}\geq x_{in_{2}}+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, since the height of <math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>τ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[v_{out,r}^{-}-\epsilon,v_{out,r}^{-}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[v_{out,r}^{-}-\epsilon,v_{out,r}^{-}]</annotation></semantics></math> is at least 1, it holds that <math id="S3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implies that <math id="S3.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and the gate constraint is satisfied. The argument for the case when <math id="S3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{2}}^{-}&gt;t_{in_{1}}^{+}-2\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ2</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{2}}^{-}&gt;t_{in_{1}}^{+}-2\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is completely symmetrical.</p>
756:<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Logic OR gate</em> <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{{\vee}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∨</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{{\vee}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)</annotation></semantics></math>. We will consider three cases depending on the position of the cuts <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{2}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{2}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, when <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&lt;0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&lt;0.4</annotation></semantics></math> it holds that <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it holds that <math id="S3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>]. Furthermore, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> it holds that <math id="S3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&lt;0.4+4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&lt;0.4+4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math>, it also holds that <math id="S3.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}},x_{in_{2}}&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}},x_{in_{2}}&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and the gate constraint is satisfied. Next, when <math id="S3.p12.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}\in[0.4,0.8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}\in[0.4,0.8]</annotation></semantics></math> then by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it holds that <math id="S3.p12.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{in_{1}},x_{in_{2}}\in[0.4-\epsilon^{\prime},0.8+4\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>Γ</mi><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}},x_{in_{2}}\in[0.4-\epsilon^{\prime},0.8+4\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> and in particular, when <math id="S3.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math> then it also holds that <math id="S3.p12.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[0.3,0.9]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[0.3,0.9]</annotation></semantics></math> and the output of the gate in unconstrained. Finally when <math id="S3.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&gt;0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&gt;0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we have that <math id="S3.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&gt;0.8+4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&gt;0.8+4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is greater than <math id="S3.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math> which implies that at least one of the two inputs is greater than <math id="S3.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the gate’s output lies in <math id="S3.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1-\epsilon,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1-\epsilon,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math> when the inputs are smaller than <math id="S3.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> or greater than <math id="S3.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and at least one of them is greater than <math id="S3.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that the gate constraint is satisfied.</p>
759:<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Logic AND gate</em> <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{{\wedge}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>∧</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{{\wedge}},v_{in_{1}},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)</annotation></semantics></math>. Analogously to the Logical OR gate, we will consider three cases, depending on the position of the cuts <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{2}}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{2}}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, when <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&lt;1.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&lt;1.4</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math> and by Inequality <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&lt;1.4+4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&lt;1.4+4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is at most <math id="S3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math> and the gate constraint is satisfied for the same reason as in the third case of the Logic OR gate above (using the argument symmetrically, for values smaller than <math id="S3.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> instead of at larger than <math id="S3.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>). Next, when <math id="S3.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.4\leq t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}\leq 1.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.4\leq t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}\leq 1.8</annotation></semantics></math>, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and for <math id="S3.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p13.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[1.3,1.9]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}\in[1.3,1.9]</annotation></semantics></math> and the output of the gate is unconstrained. Finally, when <math id="S3.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&gt;1.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in_{1}}^{+}+t_{in_{2}}^{+}&gt;1.8</annotation></semantics></math>, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and for <math id="S3.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&gt;1.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in_{1}}+x_{in_{2}}&gt;1.7</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, it holds that <math id="S3.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and the gate constraint is satisfied.</p>
762:<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Logic NOT gate</em> <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(G_{{\neg}},v_{in},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mo>¬</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(G_{{\neg}},v_{in},v_{in_{2}},v_{out},nil)</annotation></semantics></math>. We will consider three cases, depending on the location of the cut <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in}^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in}^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, if <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in}^{-}&lt;0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in}^{-}&lt;0.4</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[-\epsilon^{\prime},\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[0,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it holds that <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in}&lt;0.4+4\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in}&lt;0.4+4\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is at most <math id="S3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math> and the gate constraint is satisfied because for any value of the input smaller than <math id="S3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the output is in <math id="S3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, when <math id="S3.p14.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t_{in}^{-}\in[0.4,0.8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in}^{-}\in[0.4,0.8]</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math>, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> it holds that <math id="S3.p14.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{in}\in[0.3,0.7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in}\in[0.3,0.7]</annotation></semantics></math> and the output of the gate in unconstrained. Finally, when <math id="S3.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{in}^{-}&gt;0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{in}^{-}&gt;0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>Γ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{-}\in[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> and by Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}\in[1-\epsilon,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, by Inequality <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and for <math id="S3.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/40</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{in}&gt;0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{in}&gt;0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and the gate constraint is satisfied for a reason analogous to the one described above.</p>
765:<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Given the discussion above, by setting <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\min\{\epsilon/11,1/40\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\min\{\epsilon/11,1/40\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
769:            We can in fact assume some <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\leq 11/40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\leq 11/40</annotation></semantics></math>, as the smaller the <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the harder the problem is, since we are interested in establishing hardness for some constant <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>, the gate constraints are satisfied, and the vector <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution to <math id="S3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gcircuit</span>.
774:<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Now from Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we obtain the following result.</p>
777:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
779:<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> is PPAD-hard.</span></p>
785:<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that in the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> was constrained to be at most <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{1/40,\epsilon/11\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{1/40,\epsilon/11\}</annotation></semantics></math> and in particular by Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ([]). ‣ 2.1 Generalized Circuits ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, there exists a constant <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that would make the reduction work. Recall however that we “expanded” the instance of <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p17.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Chalving</span> from the interval <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,6N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,6N]</annotation></semantics></math> for convenience, which implies that after rescaling the instance to a constant interval <math id="S3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math>, the allowed error <math id="S3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> goes down to <math id="S3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/n)</annotation></semantics></math>. To get a constant error <math id="S3.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we simply multiply all valuations by <math id="S3.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
789:<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implies that although a solution with <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cuts is generally desirable, it might be hard to compute, even for a relatively simple class of valuations like those used in the reduction. In fact, we can extend our results to the more general case of finding a partition with <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k</annotation></semantics></math> cuts where <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant.</p>
792:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
794:<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be any constant. Then there exists a constant <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n+k,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n+k,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Chalving</span> is PPAD-hard.</span></p>
800:<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(V,\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(V,\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math> be an instance of <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p19.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Gcircuit</span> with <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> nodes, consisting of smaller identical sub-circuits <math id="S3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}=(V_{i},\mathcal{T}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}=(V_{i},\mathcal{T}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, with with <math id="S3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N/(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N/(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math> nodes each such that for all <math id="S3.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[k+1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[k+1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}\cap V_{j}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}\cap V_{j}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. and <math id="S3.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}\cap\mathcal{T}_{j}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}\cap\mathcal{T}_{j}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the circuit <math id="S3.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S3.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> copies of a smaller circuit <math id="S3.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that do not have any common nodes or gates. Furthermore, for convenience, assume without loss of generality that for two nodes <math id="S3.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p19.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{l}\in V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{l}\in V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p19.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{m}\in V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{m}\in V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p19.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the labeling of the nodes is such that nodes in circuits with smaller indices have smaller indices.</span></p>
803:<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be the instance of <math id="S3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p20.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> corresponding to the circuit <math id="S3.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> following the reduction described in the beginning of the section and recall that <math id="S3.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2N</annotation></semantics></math> in the construction. Note that according to the convention adopted above for the labeling of the nodes, for <math id="S3.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, the agents corresponding to <math id="S3.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the interval <math id="S3.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell_{i},r_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell_{i},r_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the agents corresponding to <math id="S3.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the interval <math id="S3.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell_{j},r_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell_{j},r_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}\leq\ell_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}\leq\ell_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, agents corresponding to sub-circuits with smaller indices are placed before agents with higher indices, and there is no overlap between agents corresponding to different sub-circuits.</span></p>
806:<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now suppose that we have a solution to <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n+k,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n+k,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p21.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>. Since there is no overlap between valuations corresponding to different sub-circuits, an approximate solution with <math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k</annotation></semantics></math> cuts for the instance <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> implies that there exists some interval <math id="S3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell_{i},r_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell_{i},r_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the set of nodes <math id="S3.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of sub-circuit <math id="S3.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that at least <math id="S3.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math> cuts lie in <math id="S3.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell_{i},r_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell_{i},r_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise the total number of cuts on <math id="S3.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> would be at least <math id="S3.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since there are exactly <math id="S3.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math> agents with valuations on <math id="S3.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell_{i},r_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell_{i},r_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>, this would imply an approximate solution for <math id="S3.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> agents with <math id="S3.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> cuts and the problem reduces to <math id="S3.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.p21.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>.
812:<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span><math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n-1,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n-1,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Chalving</span> is NP-hard</h2>
815:<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section, we proved that the problem of finding an approximate solution with <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> players and <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cuts is PPAD-complete. Recall that for that case, we know that a solution exists <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. For <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> players and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> cuts however, we don’t have the same guarantee. We prove that deciding whether this is the case or not is NP-hard.</p>
818:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
820:<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n-1,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n-1,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> is NP-hard.</span></p>
826:<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will first describe the construction that we will use in the reduction. For consistency with the previous section, we will denote the error of the Consensus-halving problem by <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the error of the (implict) generalized circuits by functions of <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\epsilon}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\epsilon}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> be the construction for the reduction of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that encodes an <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized circuit <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into an <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n-1,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n-1,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> halving instance when <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11</annotation></semantics></math>. We will reduce from 3-SAT, which is known to be NP-complete.</span></p>
829:<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> be any 3-SAT formula with <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> clauses, <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 3m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 3m</annotation></semantics></math> variables <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be given. For convenience of notation, let <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\epsilon/11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\epsilon/11</annotation></semantics></math>. We will (implicitly) create a generalized circuit <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with the following building blocks:</p>
860:<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Following the notation introduced above, let <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\delta}(\text{Bool})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Bool</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\delta}(\text{Bool})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{4\delta}(\Phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{4\delta}(\Phi)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{4\delta}(\text{Rebool})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Rebool</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{4\delta}(\text{Rebool})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the valuations of the agents in the instance of Consensus-halving corresponding to those sub-circuits, according to the reduction described in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In other words, based on the circuit described above, we create an instance <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of Consensus-halving where we have:</p>
874:<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">an additional agent with valuation</p>
879:<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}=\begin{cases}1,\text{if }t\in[b-18m\epsilon^{\prime}-1,b-18m\epsilon^{\prime}]\\
882:" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}=\begin{cases}1,\text{if }t\in[b-18m\epsilon^{\prime}-1,b-18m\epsilon^{\prime}]\\
888:<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> is the interval where the value of <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}^{bool}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}^{bool}</annotation></semantics></math> is “read” in the instance of Consensus-halving, i.e. the interval where the cut <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{bool}{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{bool}{-}</annotation></semantics></math> will be placed in the Consensus-halving solution.</p>
893:<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Recall Definition <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> from Section <a href="#S3" title="3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and note that as far as agent <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is concerned, any cut <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{bool}{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{bool}{-}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-18m\epsilon\leq x_{out}^{bool}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-18m\epsilon\leq x_{out}^{bool}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is a Consensus-halving solution.
896:<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
911:<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
936:<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We will now argue about the correctness of the reduction. Let <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of agents and notice that there are <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> agents that correspond to the nodes of the circuit and a single agent constraining the value of <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}^{bool}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}^{bool}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notice that since the allowed error for the sub-circuit <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Rebool}(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k},x_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Rebool</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Rebool}(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k},x_{out})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the total additive error of the agents of <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{4\delta}(\text{Rebool})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Rebool</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{4\delta}(\text{Rebool})</annotation></semantics></math> will be at most <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\delta\cdot 48m\leq 18m\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\delta\cdot 48m\leq 18m\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
939:<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, assume that there exists a a solution to <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-approximate Consensus-halving with <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> cuts. By the correctness of the construction of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 (𝑛,𝑛+𝑘,ϵ)-ConHalving is PPAD-hard ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and the fact that <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;\epsilon/11=\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the solution encodes a valid assignment to the variables of the generalized circuit <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the valuation of agent <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the output of <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy</span></p>
946:<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}^{bool}\geq 1-18m\epsilon^{\prime}-\epsilon^{\prime}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}^{bool}\geq 1-18m\epsilon^{\prime}-\epsilon^{\prime},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
949:<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">otherwise the corresponding cut <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{out}^{bool}{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{out}^{bool}{-}</annotation></semantics></math> could not be a part of a valid solution. Since the total additive error for the circuit <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Rebool}(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k},x_{out})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Rebool</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Rebool}(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k},x_{out})</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18m\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18m\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, if we choose <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/90m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>¡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}&lt;1/90m</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
956:<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{out}^{bool}\geq 4/5-\epsilon^{\prime}\ \ \text{which implies that}\ \ x_{out}\geq 3/4," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>which implies that</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{out}^{bool}\geq 4/5-\epsilon^{\prime}\ \ \text{which implies that}\ \ x_{out}\geq 3/4,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
972:<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">For the other direction, assume that <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfiable and let <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}=(\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}=(\tilde{x}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{x}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> be a satisfying assignment. First we set the values of the variables <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> according to <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and then we propagate the values up the circuit <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> using the exact operation of the gates, which by our construction can be encoded to an instance of exact Consensus Halving for the <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> agents corresponding to the nodes of <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the first <math id="S4.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ1</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> will be exactly satisfied with the partition resulting from the encoded satisfying assignment. For the <math id="S4.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th agent, again, since the total additive error is bounded by <math id="S4.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18m\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18m\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, agent will be satisfied with the solution.
977:<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Again, we remark here that by using similar arguments as in the proof of Corollary <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, we can prove that the result holds even when the valuations are probability measures.</p>
981:<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span><math id="S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> is in PPA</h2>
984:<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove that <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> is in PPA. As we discussed in the introduction, this result of ours was referenced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> to complement the PPA-hardness reduction of the inverse-exponential precision version and obtain PPA-completeness. For establishing this result, we construct a reduction from <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> to the PPA-complete problem <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Leaf</span> which goes via <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span>, the computational version of Tucker’s Lemma.</p>
987:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
989:<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-<span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> is in PPA.</span></p>
995:<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The result is a corollary of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 5.2 From Consensus-halving to Tucker ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 5.3 From Tucker to Leaf ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, which will be proved in the remainder of the section.
1000:<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Before we proceed, we provide some intuition. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, Simmons and Su designed an algorithm for finding an approximate solution to the Consensus-Halving problem given access to an algorithm that solves Tucker on a triangulated cross-polytope (the formal definitions are given below). In the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 5.2 From Consensus-halving to Tucker ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we use this algorithm directly to reduce the computational version of Consensus Halving, <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>, to the computational version of Tucker, <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span>, defined below.</p>
1003:<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then, the inclusion of <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> in PPA will follow from the the fact that <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> is in PPA. This was proven in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> where the computational version of the problem is defined on a subdivision of the hypercube, rather than the triangulation of the cross-polytope, as required for the Simmons-Su algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. For the latter problem, a proof was sketched in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, where the idea was to map a triangulated hemisphere continuously and symmetrically to the hypercube. We provide a formal proof here, but following a different approach;<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
1007:          A map between the two topological spaces or the corresponding solutions seems like the most natural approach, but formalizing the intuition seems like it could result in significant technical clutter. Our approach “moves” the technical difficulty to the definition of the “aligned with hemispheres” triangulation, which can rather be easily understood intuitively. Then, the two lemmas that establish the result are basically adaptations of the known algorithms of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 5.2 From Consensus-halving to Tucker ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> (Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 5.3 From Tucker to Leaf ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</span></span></span> we use an algorithm proposed by Prescott and Su <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> (for proving a generalization of Tucker’s Lemma due to Fan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>) and prove that it can be used to recover a solution to <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> from a solution to <span id="S5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Leaf</span>. The algorithm requires <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> to be defined on a specific type of triangulation <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, which we also define below.</p>
1010:<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>The Borsulk-Ulam Theorem and Tucker’s Lemma</h3>
1048:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Borsuk-Ulam theorem)<span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
1060:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Triangulation)<span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
1070:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Centrally symmetric and <math id="Thmdefinition2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-skeleton)<span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
1089:<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Aligned with hemispheres Triangulation)<span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
1118:<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-<span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span> reduces to <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> in polynomial time, where <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a symmetric triangulation <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with mesh size at most <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the upper bound on the valuation functions of <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>.</span></p>
1124:<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given any instance of <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>, we construct an instance of <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> based on the construction in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. We note that the coordinates of any vertex <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\in C^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\in C^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> naturally correspond to a partition that uses <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cuts on the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> interval.<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
1128:                The use of the <math id="footnote7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> interval is for convenience and without loss of generality; for any choice of the interval we could use a cross-polytope corresponding to a sphere of a different radius.</span></span></span> This is because the coordinates of any vertex <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\in C^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\in C^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n+1}|x_{i}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n+1}|x_{i}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and a partition with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cuts on <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as partitioning the interval into <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> pieces such that the length of each piece is equal to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x_{i}|,i=1,\ldots,n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x_{i}|,i=1,\ldots,n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if the sign of the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th coordinate <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is “+”, piece <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned to portion <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise it is assigned to portion <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>. This interpertation is the basic bulding block of the Simmons-Su algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
1131:<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed triangulation of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense described above with mesh size <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\epsilon/2M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\epsilon/2M</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the bound of the valuation function on any subinterval. It is not hard to verify that for any two adjacent vertices <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (denote their associated portions by <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\prime}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\prime}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\prime}_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\prime}_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively), it holds that</span></p>
1136:<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|v_{i}(O_{+})-v_{i}(O^{\prime}_{+})|\leq\epsilon/2,\ \ \text{for all}\ \ i\in[n]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>Γ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for all</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|v_{i}(O_{+})-v_{i}(O^{\prime}_{+})|\leq\epsilon/2,\ \ \text{for all}\ \ i\in[n].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
1139:<p id="S5.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">To see this, note that two adjacent vertices in the triangulation <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> can only differ by at most <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in distance, which means that the cuts corresponding to the two points <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> will define intervals that are close to each other; in particular the intervals defining the portions <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\prime}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\prime}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> will differ by at most <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in length. Since the difference in value for the two intervals could be at most <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the total difference in value could be at most <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\cdot\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\cdot\tau</annotation></semantics></math> which is upper bounded by <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, by the choice of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Symmetrically, we also get that</span></p>
1144:<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|v_{i}(O_{-})-v_{i}(O^{\prime}_{-})|\leq\epsilon/2,\ \ \text{for all}\ \ i\in[n]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>Γ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for all</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|v_{i}(O_{-})-v_{i}(O^{\prime}_{-})|\leq\epsilon/2,\ \ \text{for all}\ \ i\in[n].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
1158:Note that in case <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{l}(O_{+})-v_{l}(O_{-})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{l}(O_{+})-v_{l}(O_{-})=0</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a solution of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS2.p4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>. We claim that this labeling satisfies the boundary condition of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 (Tucker’s Lemma []). ‣ 5.1 The Borsulk-Ulam Theorem and Tucker’s Lemma ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In summary, given an instance of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS2.p4.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>, we have constructed an instance of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS2.p4.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span>.</span></p>
1186:<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\frac{\epsilon}{2}=\epsilon." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\frac{\epsilon}{2}=\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
1189:<p id="S5.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">This means that the partition corresponding to the point <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf z}</annotation></semantics></math> is a valid solution to <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS2.p5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConHalving</span>.
1198:<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now proceed to the last step of the proof, establishing the reduction from <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> to <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Leaf</span>. <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Leaf</span> is the prototypical problem of the class PPA, based on which the class is actually defined <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
1213:<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although it is not necessary for the results of the present section, for completeness, we also define the <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">End-of-Line</span> problem, based on which the class PPAD is defined.</p>
1228:<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We have the following lemma. Note that the mesh size of the triangulation is not a parameter in the statement and the reduction holds for any such size. The lemma actually reduces <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> to <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Leaf</span>, for any fixed centrally symmetric triangulation that is aligned with hemispheres, and in particular any such triangulation of the mesh size needed for Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 5.2 From Consensus-halving to Tucker ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (which clearly holds if <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is aligned with hemispheres).<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
1232:            Strictly speaking, Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 5.3 From Tucker to Leaf ‣ 5 (𝑛,𝑛,ϵ)-ConHalving is in PPA ‣ Hardness Results for Consensus-Halving" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> states that all computational problems <math id="footnote8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="footnote8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span>, parametrized by a fixed triangulation <math id="footnote8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are in PPA, as long as <math id="footnote8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is centrally symmetric and aligned with hemispheres.</span></span></span></p>
1237:<p id="Thmlemma5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed centrally symmetric triangulation that is aligned with hemispheres. Then <math id="Thmlemma5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="Thmlemma5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> is in PPA.</span></p>
1243:<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We reduce <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,T)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Tucker</span> to the PPA problem <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Leaf</span>, by using the proof by Prescott and Su <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, the authors present a constructive proof of Fan’s combinatorial lemma on labelings of triangulated spheres. Fan’s lemma says that given a symmetric barycentric subdivision of the octahedral subdivision of the <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-sphere <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and a labelling from its vertices to <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pm 1,\ldots,\pm m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pm 1,\ldots,\pm m\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, such that labels at antipodal vertices sum to 0 and labels at adjacent vertices do not sum to 0, then there are an odd number of <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices whose labels are of the form</span></p>
1279:<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work takes an extra step in the direction of capturing the exact complexity of the Consensus-halving problem for all precision parameters. While we believe that the techniques used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> can prospectively be used to obtain PPA-hardness of the problem for an inverse-polynomial precision parameter, it seems unlikely that they could applicable when the precision is constant.
1280:In that sense, our main result is not implied by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, neither can it be subsumed by modifications to their reduction, even those involving highly non-trivial alterations. At the same time, our results are useful as the hardness for constant precision established here allowed the authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> to prove PPAD-hardness of the Necklace Splitting problem, for which any hardness results were not previously known.</p>
1283:<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Going beyond the Consensus-Halving problem in its original definition, it looks interesting to consider further the effects of allowing additional cuts, and the computation of exact or approximate solutions. Concretely, one might wonder how good an approximate Consensus-Halving solution can be computed in polynomial time, given, say, two cuts for each agent or how many cuts are required to produce an approximate solution for a given precision parameter. Finally, we could consider a <em id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">query model</em>, where the agents interact with the protocol by answering value or demand queries and the goal is to find bounds on the number of queries needed to compute approximate solutions. A very similar approach has been taken recently for the related fair-division problem of envy-free cake cutting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> or earlier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> under different assumptions on the agents’ valuations.</p>
