/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:176:		0 \pm \epsilon, 	\,\,\,\,\,\ & \hbox{if $\xx[v_{in_1}]=0 \pm \epsilon \,\,\ \text{and} \,\,\ \xx[v_{in_2}]=0 \pm \epsilon$.}
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:181:		1 \pm \epsilon,  	\,\,\,\,\,\ & \hbox{if $\xx[v_{in_1}]=1 \pm \epsilon \,\,\ \text{and} \,\,\ \xx[v_{in_2}]=1 \pm \epsilon$;} \\
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:182:		0 \pm \epsilon, 	\,\,\,\,\,\ & \hbox{if $\xx[v_{in_1}]=0 \pm \epsilon \,\,\ \text{or} 	\,\,\ \xx[v_{in_2}]=0 \pm \epsilon$.}
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:187:		1 \pm \epsilon,  	\,\,\,\,\,\ & \hbox{if $\xx[v_{in_1}]=0 \pm \epsilon$;} \\
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:188:		0 \pm \epsilon, 	\,\,\,\,\,\ & \hbox{if $\xx[v_{in_1}]=1 \pm \epsilon$.}
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:196:\begin{lemma}[\cite{rubinstein2015inapproximability}]\label{lem:gcppad}
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:197:	There exists a constant $\epsilon >0$ such that $\epsilon$-\GC\ is PPAD-complete. 
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:198:\end{lemma}
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:208:%	\item In Section \ref{sec:PPADhard}, we prove that $(n,n+k,\epsilon)$-\CSH\ for constant $k$ and constant $\epsilon$ is PPAD-hard. The result is established via a reduction from $\epsilon'$-\GC, and the high level idea is the following. For each node of a $\epsilon'$-\GC\ instance, we associate two agents in $(n,n,\epsilon)$-\CSH\ and a designated interval from which the values of the node will be ``read'', given the position of a cut. For each gate of the circuit, we construct ``value'' gadgets on the Consensus-halving instance, and associate a value gadget corresponding to a gate with the interval corresponding to the output node of the gate. We establish a construction that ensures that a Consensus-halving partition of the interval corresponds to a valid assignment of values to the nodes, respecting the gate constraints. To extend the result to $n+k$ cuts, we create $k$ copies of the original circuit and apply a similar construction. Finally, we explain how the reduction can be adapted to work for valuations which are probability measures and state the implication for the complexity of the Continuous Necklace Splitting problem when $t=2$.
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:212:%	\item In Section \ref{sec:NPhard}, we prove that $(n,n-1,\epsilon)$-\CSH\ is NP-hard. We establish the result via a reduction from {\sc 3SAT}. Briefly, we will construct an instance of $(n,n-1,\epsilon)$-\CSH\ with $n=2k+1$ agents (where $k$ is the number of input variables of the {\sc 3SAT} formula); the $2k$ agents will implicitly encode a generalized circuit, implementing the value gadgets corresponding to ``AND'' and ``OR'' gates of the boolean formula. The $(2k+1)$'th agent will have value in two distinct intervals, and will only be satisfied with a partition of $n-1$ cuts, if the {\sc 3SAT} formula is satisfied and vice-versa.
/tmp/paper1609src/Preliminaries.tex:216:%		\item Finally, in Section \ref{sec:inPPA}, we prove that $(n,n,\epsilon)$-\CSH\ is contained in the class PPA. We obtain the result by a reduction to $(n,\tau)$-\tucker, triangulated on the unit-sphere $S^n$. En route to the result, we prove that $(n,\tau)$-\tucker\ on the unit-sphere is in PPA. In particular, we use a triangulation of the unit sphere that is \emph{aligned with a flag of hemispheres} and explain how a constructive proof of Fan's combinatorial lemma proposed by Prescott and Su \cite{prescott2005constructive} can be converted into a reduction to {\sc Leaf}. Then, we use the construction of Simmons and Su \cite{simmons2003consensus} to obtain a reduction from $(n,n,\epsilon)$-\CSH\ to $(n,\tau)$-\tucker.  \\
