Step 0 confirms the paper’s main hardness theorem is Theorem 1: constant-error \((n,n,\epsilon')\)-Consensus-Halving is PPAD-hard; Theorem 2 preserves this with only a constant number of extra cuts. Section 5 gives PPA membership through Tucker/Leaf, not a polynomial-time solver, while the cited Simmons–Su protocol is worst-case exponential. None of those worst-case results automatically applies to the draft’s planted distribution. Its actual certificate producer is inverse generation, and its actual recovery algorithm is exact Gaussian elimination—so the draft’s Track B labeling is the honest one.

