../1605.04472/README.md:24:An efficient algorithm exists and is disclosed: treat each `x_j^2` as an unknown and use exact Gauss–Jordan elimination, `O(k^3)`. At the shipping preset it solved 8/8 instances in 8,752 mean counted exact operations (10,152 maximum), with mean wall time 0.0069 seconds in the final run (0.0033–0.0069 seconds across two runs). That is easy with software and out of reach by hand. The compact route finds the repeated background `u_j` and exceptional difference `d_j` in each shuffled row, normalizes to `a_j+(u_j/d_j)S=c_j/d_j`, sums all 32 equations to get `S=sum a_j`, and substitutes back. It takes exactly 224 counted operations, below the 300-operation cap, but still requires substantial exact rational arithmetic without tools. The paper's Introduction identifies Buchberger's algorithm as the general mechanical method; Sections 2.1–2.2 also note efficient successive elimination once a lex basis is available.
