\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent The \emph{Consensus-halving} problem is the problem of dividing an object into two portions, such that each of $n$ agents has equal valuation for the two portions. We study the $\epsilon$-approximate version, which allows each agent to have an $\epsilon$ discrepancy on the values of the portions. It was recently proven in \cite{filos2018consensus} that the problem of computing an $\epsilon$-approximate consensus-halving solution (for $n$ agents and $n$ cuts)  is PPA-complete when $\epsilon$ is \emph{inverse-exponential}. In this paper, we prove that when $\epsilon$ is \emph{constant}, the  problem is PPAD-hard and the problem remains PPAD-hard when we allow a constant number of additional cuts. Additionally, we prove that deciding whether a solution with $n-1$ cuts exists for the problem is NP-hard. 
\end{abstract}

